! ============================================================================
!  MAGIC 8-BALL  —  random responses with random() (Standard Library)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Shows: a custom verb (SHAKE), a before handler, and random() driving a
!  switch for varied output.  Try:  SHAKE BALL  (repeatedly).
!
!  HOW THE GAME FLOWS
!    One room, the Fortune Booth, with a Magic 8-Ball. SHAKE BALL (or ROLL
!    BALL) as often as you like: each shake shows one of six answers, picked at
!    random. SHAKE anything else and nothing happens. No ending.
!
!  KEY CONCEPTS IN THIS FILE
!    • Verb 'word' * noun -> Action;  — teach the parser a brand-new verb
!    • [ ActionSub; ]                — the routine that carries out an action
!    • before [ Action: ... ]        — one object answers the action itself
!    • random(n)                     — a random whole number from 1 to n
!    • switch (value) { 1: ... }     — pick one branch by number
!    • '8//'                         — a one-character dictionary word
!
!  WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE PLAYER TYPES  SHAKE BALL
!    1. The parser finds 'shake' in the grammar table (our Verb line below),
!       matches "ball" to the eightball object, and starts the action Shake
!       with noun = eightball.
!    2. Before doing the action, the library runs the eightball's `before`
!       rule. It has a Shake: block, which prints a random answer and
!       returns true: "handled, stop here".
!    3. Because `before` returned true, ShakeSub (the generic routine) never
!       runs for the ball. SHAKE ME has no such rule, so ShakeSub answers.
!    (SHAKE on its own also works: the parser fills in the only object
!    there is and says "(the Magic 8-Ball)".)
! ============================================================================

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! STORY METADATA
! `Constant` gives a name to a fixed value. The Standard Library prints Story
! (the title) and Headline (the subtitle) in the opening banner; `^` is a
! newline inside a string.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Constant Story "MAGIC 8-BALL";
Constant Headline "^Random responses with the random() function.^";

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! STANDARD LIBRARY INCLUDES (order is mandatory)
!   Parser   — tokenises input, selects action and noun(s)
!   VerbLib  — supplies default action routines (Take, Examine, Drop, …)
! Include "Grammar" goes LAST among the library files; it must follow
! VerbLib (the library checks). Our own `shake` verb is a new word the
! library doesn't define, so its Verb line could go before or after Grammar;
! this file puts it just before.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Include "Parser";
Include "VerbLib";

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! INITIALISE — place the player in the Fortune Booth
! The library calls this routine once at startup. `location = Booth;` sets
! the starting room (required in the Standard Library). The bare string
! prints the opening line, adds a newline and returns true; the banner and
! first room description follow.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ Initialise;
    location = Booth;
    "^A Magic 8-Ball rests on a table. Try SHAKE BALL.^";
];

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ROOM — permanently lit via the `light` attribute
! The "small table" exists only in this text; it is not an object, so
! SHAKE TABLE or EXAMINE TABLE get "You can't see any such thing."
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Object Booth "Fortune Booth"
  with description "A velvet-draped booth. A Magic 8-Ball sits on a small table.",
  has  light;           ! `light` → always lit; no lamp required

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! THE MAGIC 8-BALL OBJECT
!
! Placed in Booth via `->` (direct child of the preceding top-level object).
! The identifier `eightball` can't be `8ball`: identifiers must not start
! with a digit.
!
! `name` PROPERTY — multiple dictionary synonyms let the parser accept many
! natural phrasings: SHAKE BALL, SHAKE 8-BALL, SHAKE EIGHTBALL, ROLL BALL…
! It has no `has` line: a plain object can be taken, dropped and examined.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Object -> eightball "Magic 8-Ball"
  ! '8//' is the dictionary word 8. A single character in single quotes,
  ! like '8', is a character constant, not a word, so the // marks it
  ! as a word (the compiler warns about a bare '8' in a name property).
  with name 'magic' '8-ball' 'eight' 'ball' '8//' 'eightball',
       description "A glossy black sphere with a little window in the bottom.",

       ! -----------------------------------------------------------------------
       ! BEFORE HANDLER — Shake action
       !
       ! `before` is checked by the library BEFORE running the default action
       ! for any command targeting this object.  If it prints something (and
       ! thus implicitly returns true), the default is skipped entirely.
       !
       ! We handle only Shake here; all other actions (TAKE, EXAMINE, DROP …)
       ! are not listed, so they fall through to the library's defaults.
       !
       ! Note on action name spelling: inside a `before` handler you use the
       ! BASE action name (Shake:), NOT the Sub form (ShakeSub:).  The "Sub"
       ! suffix is only for the standalone action routine defined below.
       ! -----------------------------------------------------------------------
       before [;
           Shake:
               ! `print` outputs text WITHOUT a trailing newline and WITHOUT
               ! implicitly returning — execution continues into the switch.
               print "You give the 8-Ball a shake. It reveals: ";

               ! ---------------------------------------------------------------
               ! random(N) — returns a uniformly distributed integer in [1..N].
               ! random(6) gives one of six outcomes each time it is called.
               ! Each call is independent, so repeated shakes feel random
               ! (the same answer can come up twice in a row).
               !
               ! switch (expr) { N: body; … }
               !   Evaluates expr once, then executes the body of the matching
               !   integer label.  Works like a C switch with implicit breaks —
               !   in I6, each case body ends at the next label or closing brace.
               !   A bare string literal as a case body acts as `print_ret`:
               !   it prints the string and returns true, ending the before
               !   handler (and thus ending the turn).
               !   Every number random(6) can produce has a label, so one of
               !   the six always runs and the handler always returns true.
               !
               ! TILDE (~) IN STRINGS
               !   Inside an Inform 6 string literal, the tilde character (~)
               !   is an escape sequence that produces a literal double-quote
               !   in the output.  You cannot use \" in I6 strings.
               !   So "~It is certain.~" prints as:  "It is certain."
               !   (with the quotation marks visible to the player).
               !   The apostrophe in "Don't" needs no escape: the special
               !   characters inside double-quoted strings are `~` (a quote
               !   mark), `^` (newline), `@` (accents and codes) and `\`.
               ! ---------------------------------------------------------------
               switch (random(6)) {
                   1: "~It is certain.~";
                   2: "~Reply hazy, try again.~";
                   3: "~Don't count on it.~";
                   4: "~Yes, definitely.~";
                   5: "~Better not tell you now.~";
                   6: "~Outlook good.~";
               }
       ];
       ! The `];` above both closes the before routine and ends the object:
       ! `before` was its last property and there is no `has` line.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! ShakeSub — THE REQUIRED DEFAULT ROUTINE FOR A CUSTOM ACTION
!
! Every action name used in a Verb declaration (e.g. Shake) MUST have a
! corresponding routine named <Action>Sub (here: ShakeSub).  The library
! calls <Action>Sub when no object's `before` handler intercepts the action.
!
! Why is it needed if the 8-Ball's `before` always fires for that object?
!   First, the compiler insists: a grammar line `-> Shake` with no ShakeSub
!   anywhere is a compile error ("No ...Sub action routine found for
!   action: "Shake""). Second, the player can try to shake OTHER objects —
!   SHAKE ME, for instance — that do NOT have a Shake handler, and
!   something has to answer them.
!
! In short: the 8-Ball's `before` handles the interesting case, and ShakeSub
! is the fallback for shaking anything else.
! The whole routine fits on one line: `[ ShakeSub;` header, one bare-string
! statement (print, newline, return true), and `];`.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ ShakeSub; "You give it a shake, but nothing happens."; ];

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! CUSTOM VERB DECLARATION
!
! Verb 'shake' 'roll'
!   Registers two command words as synonyms triggering the same action.
!   Players can type either SHAKE or ROLL and get identical results.
!   `Verb` is for words the library does not already define as verbs
!   (neither 'shake' nor 'roll' is a Standard Library verb). To add a
!   pattern to an existing verb you'd write `Extend` instead, after
!   Include "Grammar".
!
! * noun                  -> Shake
!   Grammar token: `*` means "start of the object phrase", `noun` means
!   "match one object from the scene".  `-> Shake` maps this grammar line
!   to the Shake action.  The library then calls ShakeSub, unless a `before`
!   handler on the matched noun intercepts first (as the 8-Ball does).
!
! You can add more grammar lines for the same verb — e.g.:
!     *                   -> Shake;   (allow SHAKE with no object)
! just add another `* … -> …` line before the semicolon.
!
! This Verb sits just before Include "Grammar". For a brand-new word like
! 'shake' the position doesn't matter (before or after Grammar both work).
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Verb 'shake' 'roll'
    * noun                  -> Shake;

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
! GRAMMAR — the last library include
! "Exits" is a small frotzsmith helper that prints "Obvious exits:" after a
! room description (the Booth has none).
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Include "Exits";    ! frotzsmith: auto-list obvious exits (see Exits.h)
Include "Grammar";
